# Line endings are an integrity concern here, not a style one.
#
# This repository's claim is byte-level auditability: a scenario is frozen by
# SHA-256, runs record the hash of the exact prompt they were generated
# against, and `mondaybench freeze --check` fails if a single byte moves. On a
# machine with `core.autocrlf=true` - the Windows default - a plain
# `git checkout` rewrites every LF as CRLF and changes those bytes. That
# happened during the monday-002 reasoning audit: reverting one evaluator file
# silently changed its hash, and it had to be restored with
# `git -c core.autocrlf=false checkout`.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree match the index everywhere, so a
# checkout can no longer alter a hash regardless of anyone's global config.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit for the artefact types the integrity checks cover, so intent survives
# even if the catch-all above is ever narrowed.
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Nothing binary ships today. These are here so that adding a fixture later
# cannot be corrupted by the rules above.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
